# English captions (seconds in the ORIGINAL recording NAF006.mp3). *word* = shown in gold.
# Cleaned by hand from TurboScribe (merged NAF006.srt). Roman Urdu stretches (06:27-13:58, 18:01-20:46) translated by hand.
# Usable: 00:00-13:58, 18:01-21:24, 22:56-33:05, 33:58-35:27. Left uncaptioned on purpose: garbled lines, the
# "tasveer-e-Rahman" passages (06:18-08:16, 10:20-11:00), the eye anecdote naming private people (21:00-25:40), the
# 25:41-26:20 lines about the Prophet ﷺ (too unclear to caption), 26:41-27:50, and 33:58-35:27 (garbled / story cut off).
CAPTIONS = [
    (0.50, 5.20, "A'udhu billahi min ash-shaytan ir-rajeem. Bismillah ir-Rahman ir-Raheem."),
    (5.70, 29.30, "*Daf'-e-Khatrat*, driving away passing thoughts: we have read this title before. This book of *Bahr-ul-Uloom*, Nizam-ul-Amal-e-Fuqara, is the timetable of those who count themselves *poor* in the way of Allah."),
    (42.00, 54.80, "Nizam-ul-Amal-e-Fuqara. All these people sitting here, who are they? They are *Fuqara*. If you listen to their book, you are called a *faqir*."),
    (56.80, 109.00, "I will read the last paragraph of page 20, on Khatrat, because in it is the *gist* of what we have been reading and listening to for a month. All that we read opens up to us at once. I will read it again, and the new people will *benefit* too."),
    (117.40, 133.50, "*Khatrat*: remember one thing. A khatra is what *comes and goes*. What is it? A bad thought came, or a good thought came, and it left."),
    (135.00, 148.00, "When you make a *firm intention* about something, it is no longer a khatra; it becomes *'azm*, a resolve. Good or bad, this can happen with both."),
    (151.60, 168.20, "But a bad thought came and we *resolved* on it: “I will do this, like this.” We became *sinners*. When he resolved, what happened? He became a sinner."),
    (169.20, 176.90, "And a good thought came and we resolved on it: then we are given its *guidance*."),
    (177.50, 189.00, "Remember one thing: a khatra comes and goes. When you make a firm intention, it is no longer a khatra; it becomes *'azm*."),
    (191.30, 202.60, "A khatra is *forgiven*: that is, the bad thought that came. But the resolve is *not forgiven*."),
    (203.40, 218.90, "When a bad thought comes and we make a firm intention, we become sinners. If there is no intention, the khatra is forgiven; but the resolve is not."),
    (219.90, 238.80, "If a person has made a firm intention to do a bad deed, but things happened so that he could not *carry it out*: this resolve is a sin. He will be punished for it, but *less* than for the bad deed."),
    (240.90, 257.00, "He did not do the bad deed; things came in between and he could not. Still he is answerable for this sin, but not as much as for *doing* the bad deed."),
    (258.10, 267.00, "Good and bad thoughts come and go from *morning to evening*; no one is held to *account* for them."),
    (303.70, 331.50, "In driving away bad thoughts there is an important principle: *nazar bar qadam*, keep your gaze on your steps. Always keep your eyes *lowered*; do not look here and there."),
    (333.30, 354.00, "There is a great *attraction* in the human form. If you look at it, its image will come into your *heart* through your eyes; if you keep looking all the time, your heart will be troubled."),
    (543.90, 556.70, "Now then: how can these passing thoughts be *driven away*? In driving away khatrat there is one important principle: *nazar bar qadam*."),
    (556.70, 571.60, "Keep your gaze on your *feet*, on your steps. And *khalwat dar anjuman*, solitude in the crowd: keep your attention in your *heart*."),
    (574.40, 590.00, "Always keep your *gaze lowered*. Always keep your gaze lowered. Do not look here and there."),
    (590.00, 608.40, "There is great *attraction* in the human face. Whoever you look at, his face will come into your *heart* through your eyes; through your eyes, into your heart."),
    (608.40, 623.70, "If you keep looking all around, your heart will be *scattered*. The human face is a *calamity*; it is a qiyamat."),
    (660.20, 672.00, "These eyes are great *hunters*. What are these red threads? A *net* that has been spread."),
    (672.70, 692.30, "The red threads in the eyes, the red lines that stay in the eyes: that is what he is speaking of. These eyes are great hunters; these red threads are a net spread out."),
    (692.30, 713.20, "If a *bird* sits in a net, what happens? It is *caught* in it. Bird or animal, whatever it is, it is caught. So what are these red threads? A net that has been spread."),
    (713.40, 733.50, "“*Laal dore hain band-e-pa-e-nazar, sayd karti hain yaar ki aankhen*”: the red threads are *fetters* on the feet of the gaze; the Beloved's eyes go hunting. They seize the gaze and hold it fast."),
    (733.50, 752.00, "*Band-e-pa*: pa means the foot; band means to seize it. The foot of the gaze is *seized*. This is hard to understand, so I am explaining it."),
    (752.00, 769.30, "Red threads, fetters on the feet of the gaze. *Sayd* means hunting: the Beloved's eyes hunt. Who is the Beloved? *Allah*."),
    (775.10, 795.40, "For a few days, *guard your gaze*. This is the practical teaching he is giving: for a few days guard your gaze, and *protect yourself* from the gaze of others."),
    (795.50, 806.90, "When the heart is fixed on *one point*, the reality will come before you. We do not meet anyone's eyes; we keep our gaze lowered."),
    (806.90, 820.00, "Then what will happen? When the heart is fixed on one point, the *reality* will come before you, and we will come to know it."),
    (1081.60, 1097.20, "…*fil-afaq*: in the horizons, in this world; *wa fi anfusihim*: and within themselves, in their hearts."),
    (1099.10, 1113.90, "So now, whichever elder you hold in your thought, the *states* that are in that elder's blessed chest will begin to *appear* to you."),
    (1114.20, 1126.00, "Allahu Akbar, what a great thing is being taught! In what *heedlessness* we have lived. I spent so much of my life in heedlessness."),
    (1126.00, 1147.30, "“Baba, does my *tasawwur* come to you?” “No, it does not.” “Then do this, Baba: read *Ya Khaliqu… Ya Musawwiru*.” Musawwir comes from *tasveer*, image."),
    (1147.30, 1161.50, "“Look at me in your tasawwur.” So I held Hazrat in my thought, and I was looking *within* Hazrat."),
    (1161.50, 1177.60, "And what happened to me? *Kashf* opened for me; kashf opened for me. When did this happen to me? Very long ago. Remember this."),
    (1177.60, 1200.40, "I had a book this thick. Its title, the name of the book, is *Muraqaba aur Kashf*: this thick, five hundred pages. When would I read it? When would you read it?"),
    (1214.60, 1232.00, "What did my *pir* do for me? “Baba, does my tasawwur come to you?” “No, it does not.” “All right, do this.”"),
    (1232.90, 1250.50, "What was he telling me? “Do this: read *Ya Khaliqu… Ya Musawwiru*.” I have told you; see for yourselves."),
    (1250.50, 1257.80, "This is what *opened my eyes*. I did not need to read *books*."),
    (1686.10, 1702.30, "After the Friday prayer I go to the *mosque*. As soon as I sit on the chair and begin to speak…"),
    (1706.30, 1722.20, "As soon as I begin, where does my gaze go? To *Allah*. I do not look at anyone."),
    (1724.40, 1742.80, "As I keep reciting, I turn to Allah. I am immersed in His *mercy*: to be saved from his evil, I come into Allah's *protection*."),
    (1746.90, 1764.20, "As soon as I say it, he gets up from the mosque and *runs* like this. Don't ask me; I have told you many times. As soon as I say it, he runs away."),
    (1766.80, 1786.10, "Allah commands: recite, and come into My *protection*, to be saved from his evil. Whose command is it? *Allah's* command. Did I not follow it? I did. Then where can Shaytan be? How can he *mislead*?"),
    (1786.10, 1797.90, "This is our *faith*. So take Allah's command from above. Do not look here and there: pay attention to *Allah*, to His attributes."),
    (1820.30, 1831.70, "Look, there is one more thing: do not eat *many kinds* of food. Do not eat many kinds of food."),
    (1834.40, 1851.50, "“Many-coloured food brings *impurity* into the thoughts.” See: many-coloured food brings impurity into the thoughts. Hazrat has put it in *verse*."),
    (1855.70, 1867.30, "“Many-coloured food brings impurity into the thoughts.” What does it mean? It brings *impurity*."),
    (1868.50, 1879.30, "When will you reach your *Murad*, your goal? Where will you find *Tawheed*? When will you reach your goal? Where will you find Tawheed?"),
    (1943.20, 1958.30, "Do you understand? Instead of thinking in *one direction*, you are thinking about *everything*. When will you reach your goal? Where will you find Tawheed? *Tawheed* is one: Allah is *one*."),
    (1959.30, 1986.00, "When will you reach your goal? Where will you find Tawheed? This is what is being said. When will you reach your goal? Where will you find Tawheed?"),
]
